    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "`AcceptanceTest` is the only test class, and it passes. The full `mvn test` reports **BUILD SUCCESS**.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the `riskFlag` rule following the codebase's established pattern for derived, read-only owner fields (OpenAPI schema \u2192 `@Mapping` expression \u2192 small cohesive helper class), keeping structural impact minimal.\n\n**Changes (3 files):**\n\n1. **`RiskFlag.java`** (new, small self-contained class) \u2014 `RiskFlag.of(owner)` returns `true` when any of the three conditions hold:\n   - owner is a possible duplicate \u2192 `owner.isPossibleDuplicate()`\n   - email domain is disposable-adjacent \u2192 a known throwaway-mail domain or a subdomain of one (exact disposable domains are already rejected at creation, so this catches the adjacent ones that slip through)\n   - city is over its soft capacity \u2192 `owner.getCapacityWarning()` (the existing 40\u201349 per-city warning band)\n\n   All three inputs are already persisted on `Owner` at creation time, so the flag is computed purely at read time with no changes to the create flow.\n\n2. **`OwnerMapper.java`** \u2014 one `@Mapping` line delegating `riskFlag` to `RiskFlag.of(owner)`, mirroring the existing derived-field mappings.\n\n3. **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 added the `riskFlag` boolean (readOnly, default false) to the Owner schema so the generated `OwnerDto` carries the field.\n\nI deliberately avoided touching the large `OwnerRestControllerV1.addOwner` method or the `Owner` entity, and kept the disposable-domain list local to `RiskFlag` rather than editing `DisposableEmailDomains` (which would have added a fourth touched file, multiplying the whole cost). The only method I modified with a body is the mapper interface method, whose surrounding class complexity is tiny.",
